<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.11.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01451</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.11.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d50da9ad-6c3b-4362-88df-c46047d4e07f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <exercise id="_8e0f0500-55dd-4440-9978-28a0af0a9367" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00001">For questions 1 – 4, use the following:</para><para id="para-00002">Function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> describes the height of a ball, in inches, after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math><emphasis> </emphasis>bounces and is defined
        by the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">What is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>? Round to the nearest integer. Think about what it represents in this situation.</para>
      </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          
          <para id="para-00004">Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3. Are you using it
        the right way?</para>
          <para id="para-00005">The correct answer is 61. After 3 bounces, the bounce height is about 61 inches.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_6d7401c6-c597-45e1-815d-25b0c4ade80f" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002"><para id="para-00006">Could <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> be 150?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          
          <para id="para-00007">Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.</para>
          <para id="para-00008">The correct answer is no. The ball is dropped from 120 inches, when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, and decreases for each bounce
        after that.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_10c85cd5-5b32-428f-922e-29567dcc1617" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003"><para id="para-00009">Which ball loses its height more quickly, this ball, modeled by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, or a
          tennis ball whose height in inches after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> bounces is modeled by the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          
          <para id="para-00010">Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3. Are you using it
        the right way?</para>
          <para id="para-00011">The correct answer is the ball modeled by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. The ball modeled by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> loses height more quickly
        because the bounce factor is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>, which is less than <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="e7b5aa97-329f-4653-b67e-535dcc64da64" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00012">How many bounces would it take before the ball, modeled by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, bounces less than 12 inches from the
          surface?</para>
      </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          
          <para id="para-00013">Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.2 – 5.11.3 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.</para>
          <para id="para-00014">The correct answer is 11.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_2f20d8bb-d3db-4e5e-b972-61d20ef47f9a" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005"><para id="para-00015">After a second bounce, a ball reached a height of 70 cm. The rebound factor for the ball was 0.8. From
          approximately what height, in cm, was the ball dropped?</para>
      <para id="para-00016">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00017">560</para><para id="para-00018">56</para><para id="para-00019">21</para><para id="para-00020">109</para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00021">Correct! 109 cm</para>
          <para id="para-00022">Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.4 – 5.11.5. Are you using it
        the right way?</para>
          <para id="para-00023">The correct answer is 109 cm.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_133a4d47-85ea-4592-98ce-f24d7e3a3aa1" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00006"><para id="para-00024">Using the table below, find the height, in cm, at which the ball was dropped.


      </para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
            <entry><emphasis>
              Number of Bounces</emphasis></entry><entry>
              1
            </entry>
            <entry>
              2
            </entry>
            <entry>
              3
            </entry>
            <entry>
              4
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>Height in cm</emphasis></entry><entry>
              90
            </entry>
            <entry>
              54
            </entry>
            <entry>
              32
            </entry>
            <entry>
              19
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      
      </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          
          <para id="para-00025">Try again. Revisit activities 5.11.4 – 5.11.5 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.
    </para>
          <para id="para-00026">The correct answer is 150.
    </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </section>
</content></document>
